    "https://trincoll.peopleadmin.com/postings/3657": {
      "title": "K.W. Davis Endowed Professor of Economic Organizations and Innovation",
      "description": "Trinity College | K.W. Davis Endowed Professor of Economic Organizations and Innovation Skip to Main Content Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help K.W. Davis Endowed Professor of Economic Organizations and Innovation Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. To email this position to a friend, click on the Email to a Friend link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Completed applications, including cover letter, CV, letters of recommendation, teaching statement, a writing sample, and official summaries of teaching evaluations should be received for priority evaluation by October 15, 2025 to receive full consideration (but will continue until filled). Video interviews will be conducted in the second half of October. Contact: Edward.Stringham@trincoll.edu . Applicants are asked to list contact information for reference providers, including email addresses. Once a completed dossier is submitted, automatic emails will be generated to each reference provider, directing each referee to a unique URL where the referee must upload a letter of recommendation. Applicants using Interfolio (or other dossier service) should provide the appropriate unique Interfolio email address for each reference letter writer. Trinity is a highly selective, independent, nonsectarian liberal arts institution located in the capital city of Hartford, Connecticut. The college maintains a rigorous academic profile complemented by a vibrant co-curricular program. With more than 2,100 full-time undergraduate students, representing forty-three states and ninety-one countries, we consider our location in a culturally and socioeconomically diverse capital city to be among Trinity’s most distinctive assets; and we cultivate strong connections with our surrounding neighbors and with institutions and organizations throughout Hartford and the region. As a preeminent liberal arts college in an urban setting, Trinity College prepares students to be bold, independent thinkers who lead transformative lives. Posting Details Position Information Title K.W. Davis Endowed Professor of Economic Organizations and Innovation Tenure Track or Non Tenure Track Non Tenure Track Faculty Rank Open-rank Professor Open Date 08/28/2025 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Full or Part Time Full Time Position Summary Trinity College seeks to hire the K.W. Davis Endowed Professor of Economic Organizations and Innovation beginning January 2026 or possibly later. We seek scholars who are committed to excellence in undergraduate teaching and research. The position is funded by the Shelby Cullom Davis Endowment for American Business and Economic Enterprise at Trinity College, which was created to foster understanding of, and promote the activity of entrepreneurship and the operation of a market economy. This is a non-tenured Named Professorship with an initial three year contract with eligibility for five year contracts afterwards. This type of position and the renewal process will be clearly specified in the appointment letter. If the initial appointment is not at the level of Full Professor, promotion is possible. Qualifications Ph.D. or equivalent. Ph.D. in Economics, Sociology, History, or related field, or equivalent terminal degree, such as J.D. Ideal candidates have experience with rigorous student centered pedagogy that develops transferable skills in composition, research, public speaking, and full and fair consideration of diverse viewpoints in the spirit of the liberal arts. A preference will be given to candidates with interdisciplinary interests. Candidates will teach four courses per year, and are eligible for a one-semester leave every fourth year. in the Formal Organizations program, based on their specific interests and area of disciplinary competence from a broad established curriculum. Sample courses include: Formal Organizations and Market Behavior, Entrepreneurship and Markets, Entrepreneurship and Society, Issues in Methodological Individualism, Exit Voice and Power in Organizations, Normative Analysis of Organizations, College, Sports Organizations, and Matching Markets. Compensation Competitive salary and benefits, plus start-up expense fund. Special Instructions to Applicants Completed applications, including cover letter, CV, letters of recommendation, teaching statement, a writing sample, and official summaries of teaching evaluations should be received for priority evaluation by October 15, 2025 to receive full consideration (but will continue until filled). Video interviews will be conducted in the second half of October. Contact: Edward.Stringham@trincoll.edu . Applicants are asked to list contact information for reference providers, including email addresses. Once a completed dossier is submitted, automatic emails will be generated to each reference provider, directing each referee to a unique URL where the referee must upload a letter of recommendation. Applicants using Interfolio (or other dossier service) should provide the appropriate unique Interfolio email address for each reference letter writer. Trinity is a highly selective, independent, nonsectarian liberal arts institution located in the capital city of Hartford, Connecticut. The college maintains a rigorous academic profile complemented by a vibrant co-curricular program. With more than 2,100 full-time undergraduate students, representing forty-three states and ninety-one countries, we consider our location in a culturally and socioeconomically diverse capital city to be among Trinity’s most distinctive assets; and we cultivate strong connections with our surrounding neighbors and with institutions and organizations throughout Hartford and the region. As a preeminent liberal arts college in an urban setting, Trinity College prepares students to be bold, independent thinkers who lead transformative lives. Posting Number F00287 Quick Link https://trincoll.peopleadmin.com/postings/3650 Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Sample of Scholarly Writing Teaching evaluations summaries Statement of Teaching Philosophy Optional Documents 300 Summit Street, Hartford CT 06106 | 860-297-2000 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://trincore-ibvxjb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/108": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant, Economics",
      "description": "Department: Economics Category: Academic Affairs (non-faculty) JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Hartford, CT, United States Responsible for providing administrative, operational and departmental support for center, academic, or non-academic department. Coordinates departmental workflow, scheduling, planning, and travel for meetings, specific departmental events, programs, and seminar series. Maintains expenditures, monitors budgets, and coordinates purchases of office supplies. Assists in various marketing efforts including, but not limited to creating and distribution of promotional materials for events such as brochures and flyers. The anticipated annual base salary hiring range for this position is $23 to $23.50 per hour, with final placement determined by experience, skills, qualifications, and internal equity considerations. High School Diploma or GED Bachelor's Degree, preferred 1-3 years’ relevant work experience or specialized training is preferred ​​Strong problem-solving skills with the ability to work well in fast-paced and stressful environments ​Good time management skills with the ability to prioritize work ​Excellent human relations/customer service skills with the ability to respond effectively to sensitive inquiries or complaints ​Demonstrated understanding of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion ​Ability to work independently as well as with a team ​Effective communication (written and oral) skills ​Public speaking and skills to develop presentations ​Ability to perform data entry with a high degree of accuracy ​Ability to evaluate information, interpret and analyze data, and develop reports ​Skill in program planning, implementation, and management ​Ability to train team members or student employees and schedule/coordinate work and activities of others ​Ability to cultivate and maintain relationships with leadership, faculty, staff, students, and external stakeholders/constituencies ​Ability to uphold a professional code of ethics and maintain confidentiality ​Knowledge of departmental technology and computing skills ​Skill in accounting and budget management ​ Acts as first point of contact for unit or department; answering questions and providing resources to students, staff, faculty via phone or email. May serve as back-up contact in related areas/departments, as needed. Manages scheduling and calendar appointments. ​​Assists with coordinating events and/or meetings including scheduling, securing locations/event spaces, arranging catering, working with vendors and presenters, identifying audio-visual needs, coordinating travel (accommodations, dining reservations, etc.), etc.​ ​​Inputs data and maintains records in department databases. Compiles, tracks, and assesses various data fields within databases, as needed by department/area. May generate reports from databases for department/area.​ ​​Completes financial transactions including but not limited to, submitting invoices, check requests, reimbursement requests, transfers, expenses reports, and student payroll along with any required documentation to the appropriate offices. Monitors departmental expenditures and budgets.​ ​​Maintains and updates websites and online calendar of events.​ ​​Orders necessary office supplies and materials for department/area, meetings, and events. Works with vendors on invoicing and purchase orders. Liaises with appropriate parties to service office equipment and troubleshoot issues.​ Prepares and send communications and correspondence based on needs and direction of those within department/area. Provides marketing and communications support by coordinating, ordering, and/or creating promotional materials for events such as brochures, posters, and flyers. Perform other related duties as assigned and based on departmental need: Maintains records and data for economics majors including major declarations, class lists, Outlook distribution lists, course enrollments, grades, and contact information. Ensures data is up-to-date and accurate. Performs ad hoc department reports as needed."
    },
    "https://trincore-ibvxjb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/107": {
      "title": "Head Men's & Women's Cross Country Coach",
      "description": "Department: Athletics Category: Athletics JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Hartford, CT, United States Trinity College, an NCAA Division III institution located in Hartford, Connecticut, is seeking an individual to serve as its Head Men’s and Women’s Cross Country Coach. The Head Coach will provide overall leadership and administration of the Cross Country programs, including setting strategic direction for all aspects of the program, recruitment and development of student-athletes, competitive success, budget management, fundraising, alumni engagement, staff supervision, and compliance with NCAA Division III, NESCAC, and Trinity College rules and regulations. The Head Coach is committed to fostering a culture of excellence that supports the academic, athletic, and holistic development of Trinity College student-athletes. Trinity College offers a comprehensive benefits package that includes medical, wellness, dental and vision, retirement savings with employer match, flexible spending and health savings accounts, disability and life insurance; paid leave and other paid time off benefits; tuition benefits for employees and their dependents; and a variety of other employer-sponsored and voluntary fringe benefit programs. The anticipated annual base salary hiring range for this position is $63,400 to $80,800 per year, with final placement determined by experience, skills, qualifications, and internal equity considerations. Bachelor's Degree 3-5 years of experience Ability to develop and maintain strong relationships with student athletes, prospective students, other coaches, leadership, faculty, and staff Ability to work independently as well as within a team Strong problem-solving skills and ability to work well in fast-paced and stressful environments Ability to maintain professional code of ethics Demonstrated understanding of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion Ability to respond effectively to sensitive inquiries or complaints Schedule and coordinate work and activities of others Strong communication skills (both oral and written) and skilled in making presentations Experience planning, implementing and managing programs Ability to manage social media posts for the team Interpersonal conflict management skills Primary Responsibilities – List up to eight major duties of the job Designs, prepares, and runs practice sessions and competition strategies in collaboration with coaching staff. Coaches student-athletes during practice, at home and away events, and tournaments. Cultivate and maintains strong relationships with student-athletes, prospective students, other coaches, leadership, faculty, and staff. Actively recruits student-athletes, including communications and networking with coaches, parents, alumni, and counselors. Adheres to all NCAA and NESCAC regulations. Performs administrative tasks related to scheduling, budgeting, fundraising, equipment and facilities management, and rules compliance. Mentors student-athletes through regular conversations regarding schedule, academic priorities, wellness, leadership, and time management. Participates in various committees on campus. Teaches physical education classes and assists with varsity game management is needed. Perform other related duties as assigned and based on departmental need."
    },
    "https://trincore-ibvxjb.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/105": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Student Leadership and Engagement Operations",
      "description": "Department: Student & Community Life Category: Student & Community Life JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Hartford, CT, United States Under the leadership of the Director of Student Leadership, Engagement, and Greek Life, the Assistant Director of Student Leadership and Engagement Operations supports the overarching mission of the SLE Office and the College. This position works collaboratively With the SLE team to advise and support the operation Of Registered Student Organizations (RSOS) including the Student Government Association. This position plays an integral role in working collaboratively with SCA to provide oversight of the Student Activities Fund (SAF). This is a residential position, on-campus residence is required. Working nights and weekends as needed and/or required. Trinity College offers a comprehensive benefits package that includes medical, wellness, dental and vision, retirement savings with employer match, flexible spending and health savings accounts, disability and life insurance; paid leave and other paid time off benefits; tuition benefits for employees and their dependents; and a variety of other employer-sponsored and voluntary fringe benefit programs. The anticipated annual base salary hiring range for this position is $44,000 to $53,800 per year, with final placement determined by experience, skills, qualifications, and internal equity considerations. Bachelor's Degree Master's Degree, preferred 2 to 3 years of work experience in higher education, student development, accounting, financial management or a related field. Strong attention to detail and able to work independently. Display excellent decision-making, time management, oral and written communication, organizational and interpersonal skills. Familiarity and level of comfort With Google Docs, MS Office, and other productivity software. Assist the Director in administering the daily operations of the Office, including providing supervisory oversight and support for the Office Assistant and the SLE student office assistants. Engage staff in opportunities for professional growth and development. Serve as primary advisor for the SGA Budget Committee and oversee its operations. Serve as co- advisor for SGA in conjunction with Director Of SLE. Serve as the primary advisor for RSOs without an assigned advisor. Work collaboratively with the Assistant Director and Student Leadership and Engagement Coordinator to develop, implement, and assess leadership development and training programs for approximately 140 RSOS. Oversee the financial process and operations of registered student organizations which includes supervising the team that manages financial records in Bantam link. Assist the Director with oversight of the Office budgets, including negotiating and preparing contracts, forwarding high cost and/or more complicated contracts for review and approval. Serve as primary liaison to the budget office. Provide oversight of Mather Hall operations, including working collaboratively with the College Operations and Facilities teams on student-centered spaces within Mather. Oversee the underground Coffee House operations. Oversees all aspects of major event planning, including working collaboratively with the Office Of Events and Campus Operations, Campus Safety, and other campus partners. Serve in the Administrator On-Call (AOC) rotation for the college, managing student crises matters such as mental health, sexual assault, substance, and facility concerns by supporting needs of the students and engaging colleagues and/or families when necessary. Develop leadership training for student leaders. Perform other related duties as assigned and based on departmental need."
